# The Atmosphere: Composition, Layers, and the Heat Budget

> A thin shell of gases shields Earth from space, stores the Sun's
> energy, and makes weather possible. This lesson profiles the air's
> make-up, its layered structure, and the heat budget that drives
> climate.

## 1. Composition of the Atmosphere

| Gas | Share of dry air | Role |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Nitrogen | 78.08% | Dilutes oxygen; feeds life through the nitrogen cycle |
| Oxygen | 20.95% | Respiration and combustion |
| Argon | 0.93% | Inert filler |
| Carbon dioxide | ~0.04% | Photosynthesis; principal greenhouse gas |
| Water vapour and dust | Variable (up to ~4%) | Clouds, precipitation; dust seeds condensation |

@chart donut title="Composition of dry air"
labels:
Nitrogen (78%)
Oxygen (21%)
Argon and others (1%)

data:
78
21
1
@/chart

[note]
The proportions of nitrogen, oxygen, and argon are almost constant up
to about 80 km; only water vapour and dust vary strongly from place to
place and day to day.
[/note]

## 2. Layers of the Atmosphere

@diagram tree id="atmosphere-layers" layout="tree" aria="Atmosphere layers and their features"
  Atmosphere -> Troposphere
  Atmosphere -> Stratosphere
  Atmosphere -> Mesosphere
  Atmosphere -> Thermosphere
  Atmosphere -> Exosphere
  Troposphere -> "Weather and clouds"
  Stratosphere -> "Ozone layer, 20-25 km"
  Mesosphere -> "Meteors burn up"
  Thermosphere -> "Ionosphere, auroras"
  Exosphere -> "Merges into space"
@/diagram

| Layer | Extent | Temperature trend | Happens here |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Troposphere | 8 km (poles) to 18 km (equator) | Falls ~6.5°C per km | Almost all weather and clouds |
| Stratosphere | Up to ~50 km | Rises with height | Ozone layer at 20–25 km; airliners cruise near its base |
| Mesosphere | Up to ~80 km | Falls again; coldest layer | Meteors burn up as shooting stars |
| Thermosphere | Up to ~600 km | Rises steeply | Ionosphere reflects radio waves; auroras glow |
| Exosphere | To ~10,000 km | Merges into space | Light gases slowly escape |

[definition]
The steady fall of temperature with height in the troposphere — about
**6.5°C per kilometre** — is the **normal lapse rate**; it is why hill
stations are cooler than the plains below them.
[/definition]

## 3. Insolation and Its Factors

**Insolation** (incoming solar radiation) is the solar energy received
at the Earth's surface. Its strength varies with:

- **Latitude** — the Sun's angle shrinks toward the poles, spreading
  the same beam over more area.
- **Day length** — longer days deliver more total energy.
- **Altitude** — thinner, cleaner air above absorbs less.
- **Distance from the Sun** — Earth is nearest (perihelion, ~147
  million km) around 3 January and farthest (aphelion, ~152 million
  km) around 4 July.
- **Atmospheric conditions** — cloud cover and dust cut transmission.

## 4. The Earth's Heat Budget

Of every 100 units of incoming solar radiation:

| Fate of the energy | Units |
| --- | --- |
| Reflected and scattered straight back to space (albedo) | ~30 |
| Absorbed by the land and ocean surface | ~51 |
| Absorbed by the atmosphere (gases, clouds, dust) | ~19 |

The surface then returns its 51 units as long-wave terrestrial
radiation, which the atmosphere mostly traps before it escapes — so
the budget of the whole Earth-atmosphere system still balances.

[important title="Albedo"]
**Albedo** is the fraction of radiation a surface reflects back to
space. Clouds and fresh snow are highly reflective; dark forests and
oceans are not. Earth's planetary albedo averages about 30%.
[/important]

## 5. How the Air Is Heated

The atmosphere is heated mainly *from below*: the Sun's short-wave
rays pass through the air with little absorption, warm the surface, and
the surface then warms the air by **conduction**, **convection**, and
emitted long-wave radiation. This is why temperature falls with height
in the troposphere.

## 6. The Greenhouse Effect

Water vapour, carbon dioxide, methane, and nitrous oxide are
transparent to incoming sunlight but absorb outgoing long-wave
radiation, delaying its escape to space.

[warning]
Without the natural greenhouse effect the Earth's mean temperature
would be about −18°C instead of the comfortable ~15°C we enjoy. The
problem is not the effect itself but its rapid enhancement by fossil
fuel emissions.
[/warning]

## 7. Quick Revision

| Question | Answer |
| --- | --- |
| Most abundant gas? | Nitrogen, 78.08% of dry air |
| Where is the ozone layer? | Stratosphere, ~20–25 km |
| Lapse rate in the troposphere? | ~6.5°C per km |
| What absorbs ~51 units? | The land and ocean surface |
| Albedo of the whole planet? | About 30% |
| Radio waves reflect off the? | Ionosphere (thermosphere) |
| Greenhouse gases? | CO2, methane, N2O, water vapour |
